course: dictionary
lesson: 4093
title: Giggle and the feelings behind it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A giggle is a quick, high, silly laugh. You often giggle when something is funny, or when you feel nervous or shy. Giggle is a verb (to giggle) and a noun (a giggle).
explain: Context helps you see which meaning is being used. «After the giggles, they worked it out: people show off when they want to be liked or noticed.» The words «the giggles» tell you it means a time when you cannot stop giggling, and the sentence shows it was a group who could not stop.
explain: Giggling is not always kind. «Stop giggling and listen» is what a teacher says when giggling at the wrong time gets in the way. Giggling at someone can also hurt them, so ask yourself why you are laughing before you start.
explain: The word can also be used in phrases. «For a giggle» means for fun, and in UK informal English, «It was a good giggle» means it was a fun time. Check the words around it to know which meaning fits.
fact: Giggle means to laugh in a quick, high, silly way.
fact: As a noun, a giggle is one laugh like that: «She let out a giggle.»
fact: «The giggles» means a time when you cannot stop giggling.
fact: «For a giggle» means for fun, and «a good giggle» (UK informal) means something fun.
fact: «A spotted hyena's giggle usually means it is nervous, excited or frustrated, not happy.» So a giggle does not always mean happiness.
exercise: «A spotted hyena's giggle usually means it is nervous, excited or frustrated, not happy.» What does this tell us about a giggle? | A giggle is a kind of growl | A giggle always means the animal is happy | *A giggle can show nerves, excitement or frustration | A giggle means the animal is going to sleep
exercise: Ruby says «for a giggle» means to laugh at someone. What does it really mean? | To do something for fun | To laugh at someone who has fallen over | *To do something for fun, not to hurt anyone | To tell a joke to a teacher
exercise: «The girls got the giggles during the quiet test.» What are "the giggles"? | A kind of shoe for running | *A time when you cannot stop giggling | A quiet test for children
exercise: Which word in «Stop giggling and listen» tells you the teacher wants the class to stop laughing? | Giggling | *Stop | And | Listen
test: «Stop giggling and listen.» Why might a teacher say this? | *Giggling at the wrong time can be rude or distracting | Listening is a kind of giggle | To ask the class to laugh more | To show the lesson is over
test: Does a giggle always mean someone is happy? | *No, a giggle can also show nerves, shyness or frustration | Only in stories | Yes, always | Only when people are asleep
exam: «After the giggles, they worked it out: people show off when they want to be liked or noticed.» Which word tells you the giggles lasted a while, and what does it mean? | "worked" means to play a game | "show" means to draw | *"the giggles" means a time when they could not stop laughing | "liked" means to hate something
